                "Welcome to Day 16. In classical twentieth-century commerce, marketing communications were structurally fragmented. Advertising agencies produced television commercials, public relations firms managed newspaper press releases, direct mail houses sent physical flyers, and retail trade teams negotiated in-store slotting allowances—all operating in complete organizational isolation. This structural fragmentation resulted in conflicting brand messages, disjointed creative positioning, wasted media budgets, and consumer confusion.",
                "In the late 1980s, Northwestern University Professor Don Schultz pioneered the Integrated Marketing Communications (IMC) revolution. IMC is the strategic, cross-functional business process of planning, executing, evaluating, and controlling all brand touchpoints across an enterprise to deliver a completely unified, coherent, and consistent message to target audiences. Rather than viewing communication tools as separate silos, IMC treats every customer interaction—from a billboard to a digital receipt or a customer service chat—as an interconnected expression of brand equity.",
